= Accuracy and precision =

== Overview ==
'''Accuracy''' and '''precision''' describe different aspects of [[Measurement|measurement]] quality.

'''Measurement accuracy''' concerns how close a measured quantity value is to an appropriate true or reference value.

'''Measurement precision''' concerns how closely repeated measured values agree with one another under specified conditions.

A measurement process can be precise without being accurate.

== Accuracy ==
In the International Vocabulary of Metrology (VIM), measurement accuracy is the closeness of agreement between a measured quantity value and a true quantity value of a measurand.

Because a true value is usually not known exactly, accuracy is generally treated as a qualitative concept rather than a quantity assigned a simple numerical value.

Statements such as "accuracy = ±2%" often actually refer to an error limit, tolerance, specification, or uncertainty rather than accuracy in the strict metrological sense.

== Precision ==
Measurement precision is the closeness of agreement among repeated indications or measured values obtained under specified conditions.

Precision is affected mainly by random variation in repeated measurement.

Precision can be quantified using statistics such as:
* standard deviation;
* variance;
* coefficient of variation;
* range;
* repeatability or reproducibility standard deviation.

The conditions under which the measurements were repeated must be stated.

== Precise but inaccurate ==
Imagine a scale that is miscalibrated so every reading is about 2 kg too high.

Repeated measurements may agree closely with one another.

That is high precision.

But the readings are systematically displaced from the correct/reference value.

That is poor accuracy.

This distinction is why repeating a measurement many times cannot automatically remove systematic bias.

== Accurate but imprecise ==
A method can also produce measurements scattered broadly around the reference value.

Individual measurements may be imprecise, while their average over many repetitions can be close to the reference value.

Whether that is adequate depends on the intended measurement use.

== Trueness and bias ==
Metrology further distinguishes '''trueness'''.

Measurement trueness concerns closeness between the average of an infinite number of replicate measured values and a reference quantity value.

'''Bias''' is an estimate of systematic measurement error.

This gives a useful conceptual decomposition:

<pre>
accuracy
↳ trueness / systematic effects
↳ precision / random variation
</pre>

The relation is conceptual rather than a simple arithmetic formula.

== Repeatability and reproducibility ==
Precision depends on the conditions under which repeated measurements are made.

'''Repeatability conditions''' keep factors such as method, operator, instrument, location, and short time interval closely matched.

'''Reproducibility conditions''' intentionally allow specified factors to differ.

A method can therefore have different precision under repeatability and reproducibility conditions.

This metrological use of reproducibility is distinct from the computational-science meaning described in [[Reproducibility]].

== Accuracy and uncertainty ==
Accuracy should not be confused with [[Uncertainty|measurement uncertainty]].

Uncertainty characterizes the dispersion of quantity values that can reasonably be attributed to the measurand based on available information.

Uncertainty evaluation can incorporate:
* random effects;
* calibration uncertainty;
* systematic-effect corrections;
* environmental effects;
* reference uncertainty;
* model uncertainty.

A small reported uncertainty can support a claim of high-quality measurement, but "accuracy" itself is not simply the uncertainty number.

== Instrument specifications ==
Manufacturers often specify performance using terms such as:
* tolerance;
* maximum permissible error;
* resolution;
* repeatability;
* linearity;
* drift;
* uncertainty.

These should not be collapsed into one generic "accuracy" value.

The relevant specification depends on how the instrument will be used.

== Examples ==
'''Target-board analogy''' — tightly clustered hits away from the center are precise but inaccurate; widely scattered hits centered on the target can be accurate on average but imprecise; tight hits at the center are both.

'''Clock''' — a clock that gains exactly 30 seconds each day can be highly repeatable but systematically inaccurate.

'''Laboratory assay''' — repeated results can agree closely while a calibration error shifts every result in the same direction.

== Epistemic notes ==
Everyday usage often uses "accuracy" to mean general quality. Technical writing should specify whether the concern is bias, uncertainty, repeatability, resolution, or precision.

